5) BURST BALLOONS

(Interval DP – Very Hard)

⚠️ You already have “Burst Balloons” in your repo but this is the FULL interview-level explanation (most people don't deeply understand it).

🔹 Problem Statement

You are given an array:

nums[i]

You may burst balloons one at a time.

If you burst balloon i, you gain:

nums[left] * nums[i] * nums[right]

Where left and right are adjacent balloons.

Return the maximum coins you can collect.

🔹 Example

Input:

nums = [3,1,5,8]

Output:

167
🔹 Why This Is Hard

Greedy does NOT work.

If you burst in wrong order, you lose maximum coins.

This is an Interval DP problem.

🔥 Trick: Think in Reverse

Instead of thinking:

Which balloon to burst first?

Think:

Which balloon to burst LAST in an interval?

This changes everything.

🔹 Preprocessing

Add virtual balloons:

nums = [1,3,1,5,8,1]

Why?
Because edge balloons need left/right.

🔹 Define DP

Let:

dp[i][j] = maximum coins for interval (i, j)

Meaning:
We do NOT include i and j.
We burst balloons between them.

🔹 Core Formula

For every possible last balloon k:

dp[i][j] =
    max over k:
       dp[i][k] + dp[k][j]
       + nums[i]*nums[k]*nums[j]

k is the last balloon burst in (i,j).

🔹 Algorithm
1. Add 1 at start and end
2. Let n = length
3. dp = new int[n][n]

4. For length = 2 to n:
      for i:
          j = i + length
          for k in (i+1 to j-1):
              dp[i][j] = max(
                   dp[i][k] +
                   dp[k][j] +
                   nums[i]*nums[k]*nums[j]
              )

5. Return dp[0][n-1]
🔹 Java Code
class BurstBalloons {

    public int maxCoins(int[] nums) {

        int n = nums.length;

        int[] arr = new int[n + 2];
        arr[0] = arr[n + 1] = 1;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            arr[i + 1] = nums[i];

        int[][] dp = new int[n + 2][n + 2];

        for (int len = 2; len < n + 2; len++) {

            for (int i = 0; i + len < n + 2; i++) {

                int j = i + len;

                for (int k = i + 1; k < j; k++) {

                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(
                        dp[i][j],
                        dp[i][k] +
                        dp[k][j] +
                        arr[i] * arr[k] * arr[j]
                    );
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[0][n + 1];
    }
}
🔹 Step-by-Step Dry Run

nums = [3,1,5,8]

After padding:

1 3 1 5 8 1

We compute from small intervals first.

Eventually:

dp[0][5] = 167
🔹 Why This Works

Because:

When we assume k is last balloon in interval (i,j),
then:

Left side and right side already solved independently.

So:

Optimal Substructure ✔
Overlapping subproblems ✔

That’s why DP works.

🔹 Time Complexity
O(n³)

Triple nested loop.

🔹 Space Complexity
O(n²)

DP matrix.

🔥 Interview Insight

Most people try greedy → wrong.

Top candidates say:

"Let’s reverse thinking and decide which balloon to burst last."

That impresses interviewers.

